this check by `accelerated_four/jobs.py --status`), then as needed the per-run
record.json, current attempt log tail, attempt_<N>_process.json and exit records,
history.jsonl tail and output state. Use the read-only Python environment via
`source accelerated_four/env.sh` ($PYTHON_BIN). Keep every write, temporary file
and cache inside cairn_new, under logs/accelerated_four/monitor/ only.
cairn_dev is READ ONLY. No permission changes, broad audits, hashes, smoke runs,
preparation, new experiments, or changes to data/model/selection formulas.

`accelerated_four/jobs.py --status` performs the real export verification:
stopped schedule (epoch limit or patience), selected best.pt consistent with
last.pt and the strict fixed85 history maximum, frozen test rows, 85-way
probabilities, metrics, predictions, per-class and confusion exports, test config
and completion marker. Exit status alone never proves success. Do not mark a run
complete yourself or manufacture output files. The loop writes final_summary and
exits only with verified success (or when all runs are complete/exhausted).
Codex CLI failures never end monitoring.

Leave every LIVE job alone even if its log has not changed for hours. If the
wrapper died, the job.lock may still be held by its live training child
(attempt_<N>_process.json). Never kill/restart live jobs because of SSH loss,
CLI timeout, polling timeout, stale epoch lines, or missing logs. The trainer
prints a train_progress line every 100 updates; one epoch is 1165 updates.
If all states are live/complete with no actionable change, stay quiet: the
daemon already writes logs/accelerated_four/monitor/check_note.json and
notifications. Optionally append one short line to
logs/accelerated_four/monitor/codex_notes.jsonl; no chat output, no questions.

You may ONLY: read logs/artifacts, run `accelerated_four/jobs.py --status`, and
file recovery requests. Do not edit any code, config, data, model, checkpoint,
history or output file. Never launch, resume or test-run training yourself.
NEVER execute accelerated_four/jobs.py --recover, --launch-reviewed, --block or
--worker, accelerated_four/launch_four.sh, or
accelerated_four/monitor/drain_recovery_requests.py, and never call the
controller's functions through Python or another wrapper: a Codex tool's PID
namespace and lifetime are scoped to this check.

For an actually FAILED job (state failed: dead process AND unlocked job.lock AND
unverified exports), inspect its recorded errors first. Then write ONE JSON
recovery request under logs/accelerated_four/monitor/recovery_requests/:

  {"arm": "<exact arm name>", "reason": "<actual observed failure>"}

Use a unique filename (timestamp plus UUID), write a .tmp file in that same
directory, then atomically rename it to .json. Do not create a duplicate request
for an arm with a pending request. Record the request path and observed failure
in logs/accelerated_four/monitor/actions.jsonl. Never request recovery for live
or complete runs. The host daemon drains requests AFTER this Codex invocation
ends and BEFORE its after-status snapshot, invoking the bounded controller
(at most three recoveries per arm, preparation errors included). It archives
checkpoints/history/configs, resumes the exact recorded config with
optimizer/update/RNG/schedule (--resume), or runs --test-only once training has
stopped; a crash between best/last publication resumes the complete newer
best.pt epoch and the history is trimmed to the resumed epoch. It never
restarts from scratch and never changes the batch size. Processed requests and
their results are in recovery_requests/processed/; interrupted requests are not
replayed. A queued request is not a completed recovery.

If a failure cannot be repaired within these constraints (e.g. the same
deterministic error after recovery), do not request recovery again; record the
observation in actions.jsonl so the exhausted/failed state stays explicit.
Never claim completion without the verifier. The daemon handles the next
2400-second check, next-check timestamp, PID, overlap lock, summary and exit.
Do not start another daemon, sleep for the interval, or create a new automation.
